      "id": "ios-swift-core",
      "name": "Swift Language Core",
      "summary": "Optionals, value vs reference semantics, protocols, generics, closures, and error handling \u2014 the language questions iOS loops open with.",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc1",
          "title": "Unwrap without the bang",
          "situation": "Whiteboard round. The interviewer points at `let name: String? = fetchName()` and asks you to print it uppercased, but says force-unwrapping is an instant fail.",
          "prompt": "Which is the safest idiomatic way to use this optional?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "Use `guard let name = fetchName() else { return }`, then use `name` non-optionally in the rest of the scope.",
            "Write `print(fetchName()!.uppercased())` \u2014 it is concise and the value is probably there.",
            "Write `if fetchName() != nil { print(fetchName()!.uppercased()) }` \u2014 the nil check makes the force unwrap safe.",
            "Declare it as `String!` instead so the compiler stops complaining about optionals."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "guard let binds the wrapped value once and exits early on nil, so everything after it works with a non-optional. Force unwrap crashes on nil; checking != nil then force-unwrapping re-fetches and still risks races. Implicitly unwrapped optionals just hide the same crash.",
          "hint": "What construct gives you a non-optional name for the rest of the function while handling nil in one place?",
          "objectives": [
            "Bind optionals safely with if let / guard let"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc2",
          "title": "Struct or class for a model?",
          "situation": "System-design discussion. You are modeling a `Playlist` with a name and a list of songs that gets passed between view controllers.",
          "prompt": "The interviewer asks: struct or class, and why?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "Start with a struct: value semantics give each owner an independent copy, so mutations in one screen cannot surprise another; reach for a class only when you need shared identity or inheritance.",
            "Always use a class: structs copy on every assignment, which is too slow for models with arrays.",
            "Always use a struct: classes are legacy Objective-C and banned in modern Swift.",
            "It does not matter: Swift optimizes both to identical machine code, so pick by naming style."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Swift convention is value types first. A struct copy is independent, which kills whole categories of aliasing bugs. Copy-on-write means Array/Dictionary/String copies are cheap until mutated. Classes are for shared mutable identity (a player, a session), reference semantics, and deinit.",
          "hint": "Think about what breaks if two screens hold the same playlist and one edits it \u2014 do you want that edit shared or independent?",
          "objectives": [
            "Choose value vs reference semantics deliberately"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc3",
          "title": "The closure that never dies",
          "situation": "Debugging round. A `ProfileViewController` never deallocates after dismissal. Its `viewDidLoad` contains: `api.fetchProfile { user in self.nameLabel.text = user.name }`.",
          "prompt": "What is the root cause and the fix?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "A retain cycle: the closure captures `self` strongly, and the API client (owned by the VC, directly or not) holds the closure \u2014 use `[weak self]` in the capture list and `guard let self` inside.",
            "The label holds a strong reference to the view controller; set `nameLabel = nil` in `viewDidDisappear`.",
            "URLSession caches the closure forever; call `URLSession.shared.invalidateAndCancel()` on dismiss.",
            "viewDidLoad runs twice in iOS 17; move the fetch to `viewDidAppear` to avoid double registration."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Closures capture referenced objects strongly by default. If the closure's lifetime is tied (even transitively) to the object it captures, neither deallocates. The capture list `[weak self]` breaks the cycle; `guard let self = self else { return }` then gives a strong temporary for the body. Delegates avoid this by being declared `weak`.",
          "hint": "Draw the ownership arrows: VC -> api client -> stored closure -> VC. Which arrow do you cut, and how?",
          "objectives": [
            "Break retain cycles with capture lists"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc4",
          "title": "Protocol with associated type",
          "situation": "Architecture round. You propose `protocol DataSource { associatedtype Item; func item(at: Int) -> Item }` and then try to write `var source: DataSource` as a property.",
          "prompt": "Why does the compiler reject the property, and what are your options?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "A protocol with an associated type (or Self requirement) cannot be used as an existential type directly \u2014 `any DataSource` still erases the associated type, so the property cannot be typed that way; use generics (`class Store<S: DataSource>`), type erasure, or redesign around a concrete requirement.",
            "The compiler rejects it because protocols cannot have associated types at all; associatedtype is only for classes.",
            "It compiles fine; `var source: DataSource` works in Swift 5 exactly like an interface in Java.",
            "Add `@objc` to the protocol and the property will compile, since Objective-C protocols have no associated types."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Associated types make the protocol generic over conforming types, so there is no single runtime layout for 'a DataSource'. Options: make the holder generic over `S: DataSource`, build a type-erased wrapper (AnyDataSource), or \u2014 often best in interviews \u2014 simplify the protocol so it needs no associated type.",
          "hint": "If the protocol is generic over its conformers, what single concrete type could the property hold?",
          "objectives": [
            "Know PAT limits and workarounds"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc5",
          "title": "Escaping closure across threads",
          "situation": "Concurrency round. `func load(_ completion: @escaping (Result<Data, Error>) -> Void)` runs work on a background queue and calls completion there. The interviewer asks what can go wrong for the caller.",
          "prompt": "What is the complete, correct answer?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "Three things: the closure is escaping so the caller must consider its lifetime (capture lists for self); it fires on a background queue so UI updates inside it must hop to the main queue; and Result must be handled for both cases \u2014 plus the API should document its callback queue.",
            "Nothing \u2014 @escaping closures are always called on the main queue by the system.",
            "Only that Result is awkward; the modern fix is to return an optional and document nil as failure.",
            "The caller must wrap the call in a Task, because escaping closures cannot be called from non-async contexts."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Escaping means the closure outlives the call: lifetime discipline (weak/strong dance) matters. Queue discipline matters equally: URLSession and GCD callbacks arrive off-main, so touching UI there is a bug that often manifests as flaky layout, not a crash. Senior answers also mention documenting the callback queue as API contract.",
          "hint": "Trace the closure's two journeys: how long does it live, and which thread is it standing on when it runs?",
          "objectives": [
            "Reason about escaping closures: lifetime + queue"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 3
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc6",
          "title": "Design a generic cache",
          "situation": "Whiteboard system design. 'Design an in-memory cache usable for any value type, keyed by String, with a max entry count.' You have 15 minutes.",
          "prompt": "Which design earns full marks?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "A generic `final class Cache<Value>` with a dictionary plus an access-ordered doubly linked list (or ordered keys) for LRU eviction, `NSLock`/actor for thread safety, a `Value` unconstrained or constrained as needed, and a clear eviction policy stated up front \u2014 with the tradeoff discussion (simplicity vs O(1) ops).",
            "A `[String: Any]` dictionary with a Timer that calls `removeAll()` every minute \u2014 simple and generic enough.",
            "Subclass NSCache and override `object(forKey:)` with generics \u2014 NSCache is generic-ready and thread-safe.",
            "Use UserDefaults with one key per entry \u2014 it persists for free and handles eviction via the OS."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Interviewers grade the tradeoff narration, not just the code: why a class (shared identity) vs struct, why a lock or actor around the dictionary (dictionaries are not thread-safe), how eviction stays O(1), and what happens under memory pressure (hook `didReceiveMemoryWarning` / NSCache's auto-eviction as the discussed alternative).",
          "hint": "Start by stating eviction policy and thread-safety plan before writing a line \u2014 the narration is half the grade.",
          "objectives": [
            "Design generic components with stated tradeoffs"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 3
        }
      ]
