          "situation": "UIKit round. The interviewer asks where to start a network fetch that should refresh every time the screen appears, and where one-time view setup goes.",
          "prompt": "What is the correct split?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "One-time setup (add subviews, constraints, static styling) in `viewDidLoad`; repeating work (refresh fetches, re-applying dynamic state, starting animations) in `viewWillAppear` \u2014 because viewDidLoad runs once per controller lifetime while viewWillAppear runs on every appearance including back-navigation.",
            "Do everything in `viewDidLoad` \u2014 it runs every time the view appears, so it covers refreshes too.",
            "Do everything in `viewDidAppear` \u2014 earlier methods run before the view exists.",
            "Put setup in `init` and fetches in `deinit` to balance the lifecycle symmetrically."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "The lifecycle order is loadView -> viewDidLoad -> viewWillAppear -> viewDidLayoutSubviews -> viewDidAppear, then disappear in reverse. Code placed in the wrong hook either runs too often (duplicate observers, double fetches) or too rarely (stale data after popping back). Name the order cold \u2014 interviewers check it.",
          "hint": "Which of these methods fires once per controller, and which fires every time the user navigates back to the screen?",
          "objectives": [
            "Place work in the correct lifecycle hook"
