          "title": "State vs Binding",
          "situation": "SwiftUI round. A parent view owns a `Bool` for showing a sheet; the sheet's content needs to toggle it off.",
          "prompt": "How do you wire the state correctly?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "Parent declares `@State private var isPresented`, passes `$isPresented` into the child, and the child declares `@Binding var isPresented` \u2014 single source of truth lives in the parent; the child only borrows write access.",
            "Both declare `@State var isPresented` \u2014 SwiftUI syncs same-named state automatically.",
            "@Binding in the parent and @State in the child \u2014 bindings own the storage.",
            "Use a global `static var` \u2014 it is visible to both views with no plumbing."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "@State owns storage private to a view; @Binding is a reference to someone else's state. Lifting state up (parent owns, child binds) is the core SwiftUI data-flow pattern, alongside @ObservedObject/@StateObject for reference-type models and @Environment for cross-cutting values.",
          "hint": "Who should OWN the boolean, and what does the child need \u2014 ownership or a reference to it?",
          "objectives": [
            "Apply single-source-of-truth state flow"
