          "prompt": "What is the correct explanation?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "Parent proposes a size to the child; the child chooses its own size within the proposal; the parent places the child in its coordinate space \u2014 and a child can only choose sizes the proposal allows, which is why fixed frames beat flexible stacks in fights.",
            "Each view measures itself first, then tells the parent how big it is, then the parent shrinks to fit \u2014 bottom-up like UIKit's sizeToFit.",
            "SwiftUI uses Auto Layout constraints under the hood, so the Cassowary solver decides sizes identically to UIKit.",
            "Views have no say: the parent assigns frames directly, like UIKit's layoutSubviews."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "This three-step dance explains otherwise-mysterious behavior: why a `Text` in an `HStack` truncates (proposal too small), why `GeometryReader` is greedy (it takes all proposed space), and why `.fixedSize()` exists (lets the child ignore the proposal and use its ideal size). Teach the algorithm, then derive the behaviors.",
          "hint": "Who moves first in the negotiation \u2014 parent or child \u2014 and what power does each side actually have?",
