      "id": "ios-concurrency",
      "name": "Concurrency on iOS",
      "summary": "GCD, Operation queues, async/await, actors, and main-thread discipline \u2014 from dispatch basics to cancellation design.",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc13",
          "title": "Why the main thread?",
          "situation": "Fundamentals round. 'Why must UI updates happen on the main thread?'",
          "prompt": "What is the accurate explanation?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "UIKit is not thread-safe: its classes assume serialized access from the main thread, so mutating views from a background queue causes race conditions \u2014 corrupted layout, missed draws, intermittent crashes. The main thread also runs the run loop that processes touch events and screen refreshes.",
            "The main thread is simply faster \u2014 background queues are throttled by the OS.",
            "It is a convention from Objective-C with no technical basis in Swift.",
            "Background threads cannot allocate memory, and views need allocation."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "UIKit (and AppKit before it) was designed single-threaded; thread-safety was traded for performance. The rule is absolute: read or write any UIView/UIViewController off-main and you have a latent bug. URLSession completions, GCD callbacks, and async continuations all arrive off-main unless you hop back.",
          "hint": "What breaks when two threads mutate the same view tree with no locks \u2014 and why did Apple choose not to add those locks?",
          "objectives": [
            "State the main-thread rule and its reason"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc14",
          "title": "async vs sync dispatch",
          "situation": "GCD round. You need to run image decoding off the main thread, then update the UI.",
          "prompt": "Which dispatch pattern is correct?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "`DispatchQueue.global(qos: .userInitiated).async { let img = decode(); DispatchQueue.main.async { self.imageView.image = img } }` \u2014 work off-main, hop back for UI; never `sync` from the main thread onto a serial queue you might already be on.",
            "`DispatchQueue.main.sync { decode image; update UI }` \u2014 sync guarantees ordering.",
            "`DispatchQueue.global().sync { ... }` from the main thread \u2014 sync is safer than async.",
            "Run decoding on the main queue with `.userInteractive` QoS \u2014 the highest QoS prevents jank."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "async returns immediately (no blocking); sync blocks the caller until the block finishes \u2014 syncing from main to main deadlocks instantly, and syncing to a serial queue from its own block does too. QoS (.userInitiated for user-waiting work, .utility/.background for prefetch) tells the system how to prioritize, not which thread.",
          "hint": "What happens to the calling thread under sync, and what does that imply if the caller is the main thread?",
          "objectives": [
            "Dispatch async correctly across queues"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc15",
          "title": "Three fetches, one screen",
          "situation": "GCD round. The profile screen needs user, posts, and followers from three endpoints before rendering.",
          "prompt": "How do you coordinate them?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "Enter a `DispatchGroup` before each request, `leave` in each completion, and `notify(queue: .main)` to render \u2014 or modernize with `async let` / TaskGroup; either way, handle partial failure explicitly instead of hanging on a missing leave.",
            "Chain them: fetch user, in its completion fetch posts, in its completion fetch followers \u2014 simple and ordered.",
            "Fire all three and update the UI in whichever completion lands first.",
            "Use a `DispatchSemaphore` with value 0 and wait on the main thread until all three signal."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "DispatchGroup is the classic 'many asyncs, one continuation' primitive \u2014 but every enter needs exactly one leave or notify never fires (a hang, not a crash, which is why it is insidious). Modern Swift prefers structured concurrency (`async let`), which the compiler helps keep balanced. Never semaphore-wait on main.",
          "hint": "What breaks if one completion path forgets its matching call \u2014 and how does the failure manifest?",
          "objectives": [
            "Coordinate parallel work safely"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc16",
          "title": "Shared mutable cache",
          "situation": "Modern concurrency round. Multiple tasks read/write a `[String: Data]` cache concurrently and it occasionally corrupts.",
          "prompt": "What is the right fix in modern Swift?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "Isolate the dictionary in an `actor`: all access serializes through the actor, the compiler enforces isolation (no direct access from outside), and call sites use `await` \u2014 replacing the old serial-queue/barrier pattern with a compile-time guarantee.",
            "Mark the dictionary `nonisolated` so all tasks can reach it quickly.",
            "Wrap every access in `Task { }` \u2014 unstructured tasks serialize automatically.",
            "Make it a global `var` \u2014 globals are implicitly thread-safe in Swift 6."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "Actors turn 'remember to use the queue' (a runtime discipline) into a compile-time rule: the type system rejects unsynchronized access. Mention the old GCD answer too (serial queue or barrier on a concurrent queue) \u2014 interviewers like hearing you know both eras. Also note actors are reentrant: awaits inside can interleave.",
          "hint": "What did the serial-dispatch-queue pattern guarantee, and what does the actor add on top of it?",
          "objectives": [
            "Isolate shared state with actors"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc17",
          "title": "The deadlock on main",
          "situation": "Debugging round. The app freezes. The main thread's stack shows `DispatchQueue.main.sync` called from a block already running on the main queue.",
          "prompt": "Explain the deadlock and the general rule.",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "`sync` blocks the caller until the block executes; the block is queued on main, but main is blocked waiting \u2014 circular wait, permanent freeze. Rule: never `sync` onto the current queue (especially main); use `async`, or check `Thread.isMainThread` and branch.",
            "The main queue is concurrent, so the block should have run \u2014 this must be an OS bug; file a radar.",
            "sync always runs the block inline on the calling thread, so this cannot deadlock; the freeze is elsewhere.",
            "The fix is `DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now())` \u2014 the delay breaks the cycle."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "This is the canonical GCD interview trap: sync means 'wait'. Waiting on yourself is a textbook circular wait. The asyncAfter 'fix' only masks it. Senior follow-up: the same deadlock happens syncing onto any serial queue from a block already executing on it.",
          "hint": "If sync means 'block until done' and the work is queued behind the blocked caller, who runs it?",
          "objectives": [
            "Diagnose sync-dispatch deadlocks"
          ],
          "mins": 6,
          "difficulty": 3
        },
