    {
      "id": "ios-persistence",
      "name": "Persistence on iOS",
      "summary": "UserDefaults, Codable, Core Data, SwiftData, and Keychain \u2014 climbing the persistence ladder and defending each choice.",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "ios-interview-sc19",
          "title": "Where does the auth token go?",
          "situation": "Security round. The app gets a JWT after login and needs it across launches.",
          "prompt": "Where should it be stored?",
          "evidence": [],
          "choices": [
            "In the Keychain (via the Security framework / a thin wrapper): it is encrypted, access-controlled by biometry/passcode policy, and not exposed in backups the way files are \u2014 UserDefaults is a plaintext plist and the wrong home for secrets.",
            "UserDefaults \u2014 it persists across launches and takes one line of code.",
            "A global `static var` \u2014 simple and fast, and it survives backgrounding.",
            "Hardcode it in the binary \u2014 attackers cannot read compiled code."
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "why": "UserDefaults is an unencrypted plist \u2014 fine for settings, flags, and small preferences; never for tokens, passwords, or PII. Keychain items carry access groups, accessibility levels (e.g. unlocked-only), and survive app reinstall semantics differently. Say the tradeoff out loud: Keychain API is clunkier, so teams wrap it once.",
          "hint": "What does the storage look like to someone who copies the app's sandbox off a jailbroken device?",
          "objectives": [
            "Store secrets in Keychain, settings in UserDefaults"
          ],
