  "lessons": [
    {
      "domain_id": "ios-interview",
      "title": "iOS Engineering Interview Prep \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Interview loops test four things: Swift language precision (optionals, value/reference, closures), framework depth (lifecycle, layout, state), concurrency discipline (queues, actors, main thread), and judgment (tradeoffs, scoping, debugging method). Every answer should touch the 'why', not just the 'what'.",
        "Optionals are the language's null-safety contract: bind with guard/if let, chain with ?., default with ??, and treat every force unwrap as a crash you scheduled. Implicitly unwrapped optionals are for outlets and migration, not convenience.",
        "Memory is deterministic: ARC frees at zero strong references, so your job is managing the graph \u2014 weak delegates, capture lists in escaping closures, and the discipline to draw ownership arrows when something leaks.",
        "Concurrency has two eras and you must speak both: GCD (queues, QoS, groups, barriers) and structured Swift concurrency (async/await, Task, actors, MainActor). The invariant across both: UI on main, shared mutable state isolated, cancellation designed in.",
        "Persistence is a ladder, not a favorite: UserDefaults for settings, Codable files for documents, Core Data/SwiftData for queryable graphs, Keychain for secrets. Choose by query needs and threat model, and say so out loud.",
        "The meta-skill is narration: state assumptions, name tradeoffs, timebox, and verify. Interviewers hire the engineer who reasons visibly \u2014 the code is evidence, the thinking is the product."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "swift",
        "code": "final class FeedLoader {\n    private var task: Task<Void, Never>?\n    func load(for query: String) {\n        task?.cancel()                       // cancel stale work\n        task = Task { [weak self] in\n            try? await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: 300_000_000)  // debounce\n            guard !Task.isCancelled else { return }\n            let items = await self?.fetch(query) ?? []\n            await MainActor.run { self?.render(items) }      // UI on main\n        }\n    }\n}",
        "walkthrough": "One snippet, four interview themes: cancellation before new work (no stale writes to reused rows), debounce via cancellable sleep, weak self in the escaping task, and the MainActor hop for UI. If you can narrate each line's 'why', you are ready for the concurrency round."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: force-unwrapping 'because it is never nil' \u2014 interviewers treat every ! as a scheduled crash.",
        "Common trap: touching UIKit from a URLSession completion handler without hopping to main \u2014 flaky layout, not a clean crash.",
        "Common trap: answering 'which is better, UIKit or SwiftUI?' with a winner instead of tradeoffs plus a measurement plan.",
        "Common trap: storing tokens in UserDefaults because the Keychain API is verbose \u2014 say the threat model out loud."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Labs tab."
    },
    {
      "title": "From Static Diagrams to Living Systems",
      "domain_id": "systems-anatomy",
      "concepts": [
        "A textbook diagram shows you one part in isolation \u2014 a living systems view shows you how all parts of Ios Interview move together in sequence",
        "The four-step systems anatomy method: (1) See the whole in context, (2) Cut it open to reveal layers, (3) Walk through the process step-by-step, (4) Pull apart into labeled pieces",
        "Apply this to any system in this course: start with the trigger action, trace through every component it touches, watch the data flow",
        "Interview/exam power: when asked 'how does X work?', narrate the journey \u2014 'it starts here, then...'. This demonstrates senior-level systems thinking"
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "TRIGGER event occurs\n  \u2192 First system receives it (see the handoff)\n  \u2192 Processing happens in sequence (watch each step)\n  \u2192 State changes propagate (follow the ripple)\n  \u2192 Final outcome delivered\n\nEach arrow is a living handoff, not a static box.",
        "walkthrough": "This is the 'jet engine cutaway' for your field: instead of memorizing isolated facts, you see the entire flow as a moving sequence. Practice narrating this journey out loud."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Memorizing terms without understanding the processes they participate in",
        "Drawing architecture diagrams as boxes without showing the data flow between them",
        "Answering 'how does X work?' with a definition instead of walking through the sequence"
      ],
      "try_it": "Pick any process in this course. Write out the full journey from trigger to outcome, naming every system touched."
    },
    {
      "title": "Anatomy of Swift Language Core",
      "domain_id": "systems-anatomy",
      "concepts": [
        "Swift Language Core is a multi-step process within Swift Language Core: trace the full sequence from initiation to completion",
        "See it as a journey: identify the trigger, follow each handoff, watch state change at every step",
        "Every step depends on the previous \u2014 a failure at any point breaks the chain. Map these dependencies",
        "Connect Swift Language Core to the broader Swift Language Core ecosystem \u2014 nothing operates in isolation"
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Step 1: Initiation\n  Swift Language Core begins \u2014 what triggers it?\n\nStep 2: Processing\n  Data flows through \u2014 what transforms it?\n\nStep 3: Handoff\n  Result passes to next system \u2014 what is the interface?\n\nStep 4: Completion\n  Final state reached \u2014 how do you verify?",
        "walkthrough": "Apply the four-step anatomy method to Swift Language Core. Draw it as a flowing sequence, not a static diagram."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Treating Swift Language Core as an isolated component instead of part of a living flow",
        "Skipping the dependency mapping \u2014 not asking 'what does this step need from the previous?'",
        "Memorizing the steps without understanding WHY each one exists"
      ],
      "try_it": "Whiteboard the full Swift Language Core flow from memory. Label every handoff and dependency."
    },
